From: NeoDarwin
Subject: [PATCH] arm64, pexpert: identify generic Arm CPUs by MPIDR Aff2:Aff1:Aff0

DynamIQ cores (Cortex-A55/A7x/X, as on the Radxa Dragon Q8B) number
themselves in Aff1 with Aff0 = 0: the Q8B's MPIDRs are 0x000-0x700.
The cpu node's reg, which the kernel keeps as cpu_phys_id, is the MPIDR's
Aff2:Aff1:Aff0 (docs/kernel/dt-abi.md). Without HAS_CLUSTER three
lookups used less of it; on GENERIC_ARM64_PLATFORM:

- start.s: the reset vector matches MPIDR & 0xFFFFFF to cpu_phys_id, not
  MPIDR & 0xFF. With Aff0 alone every Q8B core matched the boot CPU and
  ran on its cpu_data;
- ml_get_cpu_number() compares Aff2:Aff1:Aff0, not Aff1:Aff0;
- pe_fiq.c find_gicr_pe_base() matches GICR_TYPER's Aff2:Aff1:Aff0.

The MT bit (24), which DynamIQ cores set, is outside the mask.

Rebase-risk: low (three masks).
---
--- a/osfmk/arm64/start.s	2026-09-29 09:57:18
+++ b/osfmk/arm64/start.s	2026-09-29 09:58:14
@@ -145,6 +145,14 @@
 	mrs		x15, MPIDR_EL1						// Load MPIDR to get CPU number
 #if HAS_CLUSTER
 	and		x0, x15, #0xFFFF					// CPU number in Affinity0, cluster ID in Affinity1
+#elif GENERIC_ARM64_PLATFORM
+	/*
+	 * Generic Arm: the cpu node's reg (cpu_phys_id) is MPIDR Aff2:Aff1:Aff0.
+	 * DynamIQ cores number themselves in Aff1 with Aff0 = 0 (the Radxa
+	 * Dragon Q8B: 0x000-0x700), so Aff0 alone would match every core to the
+	 * boot CPU's data. Bit 24 (MT) is not part of the id.
+	 */
+	and		x0, x15, #0xFFFFFF
 #else
 	and		x0, x15, #0xFF						// CPU number is in MPIDR Affinity Level 0
 #endif
--- a/osfmk/arm64/machine_routines.c	2026-09-29 09:57:18
+++ b/osfmk/arm64/machine_routines.c	2026-09-29 09:58:14
@@ -1388,7 +1388,12 @@
 int
 ml_get_cpu_number(uint32_t phys_id)
 {
+#if GENERIC_ARM64_PLATFORM
+	/* The cpu node's reg is MPIDR Aff2:Aff1:Aff0 (start.s). */
+	phys_id &= MPIDR_AFF2_MASK | MPIDR_AFF1_MASK | MPIDR_AFF0_MASK;
+#else
 	phys_id &= MPIDR_AFF1_MASK | MPIDR_AFF0_MASK;
+#endif
 
 	for (unsigned i = 0; i < topology_info.num_cpus; i++) {
 		if (topology_info.cpus[i].phys_id == phys_id) {
